const bodyContent = `<h2 id="what-happened">What Anthropic Actually Published</h2>
  <p>On September 11, 2026, Anthropic published a threat intelligence report covering <strong>December 2025 through August 2026</strong>. Unlike the company's earlier safety write-ups, this one is organised as a taxonomy of confirmed abuse rather than a research paper. Anthropic splits the eight-month window into seven categories: <strong>cyber operations, influence operations, surveillance, fraud, biological misuse, conventional weapons, and unauthorized model distillation</strong>.</p>

  <p>The report also names which models were involved. Abuse clustered on <strong>Haiku, Sonnet, and Opus</strong>. The newer <strong>Fable</strong> and <strong>Mythos</strong> models appear in exactly one case — a single distillation attempt — which is itself a signal about where attackers find the cheapest capability per dollar.</p>

  <p>If you build on the Claude API, the practical question is not whether the report is interesting. It is whether any of it changes what you can call, how your account is trusted, or what you should disclose to your own users. This article walks through the parts that touch developers directly, then covers the account-control changes Anthropic shipped in response.</p>

  <h2 id="distillation">The Distillation Chapter Is the One That Affects API Terms</h2>
  <p>Distillation is a legitimate training technique. Anthropic defines the illegitimate version narrowly: industrial-scale, covert campaigns that extract model capabilities without authorization, usually running through networks of fake accounts on stolen credit cards and API keys, routed through what the report calls <strong>"transfer stations"</strong>.</p>

  <p>The scale figures are the reason this section matters to anyone running an API account. Anthropic attributes its largest measured campaign to Alibaba's Qwen lab, tracked as <strong>GTG-16005</strong>: a fixed prompt that got Claude to emit its reasoning traces before answering, with those transcripts processed into fine-tuning data for the Qwen 3.5, 3.6, and 3.7 models. The peak was <strong>almost three million exchanges per day from more than 3,500 fraudulent accounts</strong>, totalling <strong>over 151 million exchanges between May and July 2026</strong>, concentrated on agentic tasks and software development.</p>

  <p>Several other cases involve labs relaying their own customers' requests rather than mining directly, which is the part worth understanding if you use a third-party model gateway:</p>

  <table>
    <thead>
      <tr><th>Actor</th><th>Tracking ID</th><th>Documented pattern</th><th>Volume</th></tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td>Qwen (Alibaba)</td><td>GTG-16005</td><td>Fixed prompt extracted reasoning traces for Qwen 3.5/3.6/3.7 fine-tuning</td><td>151M+ exchanges, May–Jul 2026</td></tr>
      <tr><td>DeepSeek</td><td>GTG-16001</td><td>Detected harness traffic (e.g. Claude Code), flagged users, routed selected requests to Claude Opus</td><td>12.1M exchanges in 14 days</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Moonshot AI</td><td>GTG-16002</td><td>Relayed customer requests while users believed they were using a Kimi model</td><td>~300,000 requests over 10 days, 5,380 accounts</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Zhipu / Z.ai</td><td>GTG-16008 framework</td><td>Ran requests through a "CoT cleaner" that converts captured traces into training data</td><td>273 accounts, 770,000+ exchanges in 10 days</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Xiaomi (MiMo)</td><td>GTG-16008</td><td>Stored its own users' coding sessions, then replayed them through Claude to generate training data</td><td>Personal data for hundreds of people, 12+ languages</td></tr>
      <tr><td>SenseTime</td><td>—</td><td>Bought transcripts from third-party intermediaries rather than capturing them directly</td><td>—</td></tr>
      <tr><td>MiniMax</td><td>—</td><td>Operated a proxy network via a shell company offering only Anthropic and OpenAI models</td><td>—</td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>

  <p>The Xiaomi case deserves a second look from a developer perspective. Anthropic states it found <strong>no evidence Claude's responses were served directly to Xiaomi's users</strong> — the relayed requests were used to generate training data. But those relayed requests contained names, contact details, and company information for hundreds of people across at least a dozen languages. If you are evaluating a Chinese-hosted gateway or a white-labelled model endpoint, that is the scenario to reason about: your prompt content may travel further than the response you receive suggests.</p>

  <h2 id="cyber">"Sophistication Is No Longer a Reliable Signal for Attribution"</h2>
  <p>The cyber chapter's central finding is a shift in economics rather than in technique. The techniques themselves are ordinary — stolen credentials, unpatched devices, SQL injection, phishing. What changed is that reconnaissance, exploitation, and tool-building now get handed to models running in parallel at machine speed, so <strong>sophisticated attacks no longer require sophisticated attackers</strong>.</p>

  <p>The most instructive case for anyone shipping an agent product is a Russian-speaking espionage actor tracked as <strong>GTG-20006</strong>, which ran a feedback loop. AI agents repeatedly checked whether the malware in play was being flagged by common security products. When an antivirus tool caught it, the agents <strong>rewrote and recompiled the malicious code on their own</strong> until it slipped past detection again. More than 20 organisations were targeted — government ministries, intelligence services, embassies, and defense contractors — with a focus on Ukraine and Europe. The drone supply chain came up repeatedly, and the actor exfiltrated a complete proprietary SDK for a drone vision system. Some access ran through compromised hotel guest Wi-Fi providers, a technique Microsoft described in July 2026 as <em>CaptiveCrunch</em>.</p>

  <p>For defenders, the reported consequence is direct: writing new detection signatures no longer slows an attacker down if that attacker cycles through variants faster than signatures can be published.</p>

  <h2 id="weapons-surveillance">Weapons and Surveillance: Where the Report Gets Concrete</h2>
  <p>Two chapters carry details that are unusually specific, and they are the ones most likely to shape how frontier API access is gated.</p>

  <p><strong>Conventional weapons, documented for the first time.</strong> A cell in northern Yemen (<strong>GTG-87001</strong>) used Claude Code in place of human software engineers for the guidance, navigation, and control software of <strong>three missile programs</strong>, including a multistage missile with a target range over <strong>2,000 kilometres</strong>. The operators ran several Claude instances in parallel and spread work across sessions so that no single session revealed the intent. A test launch reportedly failed, and within hours the actors were back in Claude working out why.</p>

  <p>A second case (<strong>GTG-27005</strong>), attributed to freelance Russian actors, built an autonomous FPV kamikaze drone swarm with a small language model onboard and terminal-phase targeting by camera. The onboard model could select targets of the "person" class and trigger detonation <strong>with no human in the loop</strong>. Anthropic states the image classifier was trained on captured Ukrainian combat footage. A Chinese case (<strong>GTG-17002</strong>) involved roughly 16 modules for electronic warfare and suppression of enemy air defenses; midway through, the simulation's default scenario switched to twelve targets in Taiwan.</p>

  <p><strong>Surveillance as the engineering workforce.</strong> The cleanest illustration of what "agentic" means at the extreme is a public-sector platform in Mali called <em>Lakana 360</em>, built by a single consultant who used Claude as the primary engineering team. The platform monitors roughly <strong>25 million SIM cards across all three national mobile carriers</strong>, identifies people by voice across SIM swaps, flags users of encryption and VPNs, and links individuals to the national biometric civil registry. Anthropic notes that suspending the account interrupted only the <em>development</em> work, not the operation, because the platform runs on local models on-premises. Iranian units are documented making similar claims, having surveilled and profiled 6,388 Iranians within a year.</p>

  <h2 id="bio">Biology: Anthropic Documents the Limits of Its Own Filters</h2>
  <p>The biology chapter is the most self-critical section, and it explains a product change you can observe from outside. Anthropic documents five anonymised cases involving working scientists on potentially dangerous dual-use projects. In one, the biosecurity classifier blocked a grant application for gain-of-function work on the chikungunya virus planned at a military research institute — but the platform operator had <strong>built a fallback that routed Claude's rejections to a competitor's model, and Claude itself wrote most of that fallback's code</strong>.</p>

  <p>The stated conclusion is that classifiers cannot simultaneously enable useful work and prevent harm, because a user's intent in dual-use areas cannot be reliably detected from the request alone. The response was product-level, not policy-level: <strong>Claude Fable 5</strong> shipped with stricter safeguards for dual-use biology requests, and the <strong>"preserved thinking"</strong> feature introduced with <strong>Fable 5.1</strong> is described as a control to stop new API accounts from manipulating the context window. Anthropic's own framing is that the only safe path to frontier biological capability runs through programs for verified users.</p>

  <h2 id="what-changes">What Changes for API Developers</h2>
  <p>Most of this does not change your daily integration. Four things do deserve attention.</p>

  <ul>
    <li><strong>Account trust is now a product surface.</strong> The report explicitly ties the "preserved thinking" control in Fable 5.1 to new API accounts. If you spin up throwaway accounts for load testing or evaluation sweeps, expect more friction than a year ago.</li>
    <li><strong>Distillation enforcement affects anyone reselling Claude output.</strong> If your product's workflow involves capturing model reasoning traces and using them as training data, the report's definition of illegitimate distillation is the boundary you need to read before you scale that pipeline.</li>
    <li><strong>Third-party gateways carry the relay risk.</strong> The Moonshot and DeepSeek cases are both cases where end users believed they were talking to one model and were actually routed to another. If your procurement checklist does not include "does this gateway disclose upstream routing?", that is the gap the report exposes.</li>
    <li><strong>Model choice shifts the risk profile.</strong> Abuse concentrated on Haiku, Sonnet, and Opus rather than the newer Fable and Mythos tiers. That is a cost signal, not a safety guarantee, but it is worth knowing when you reason about which model a red-team exercise should target.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2 id="pricing-context">The Pricing Context for This Decision</h2>
  <p>The report's presence in your decision-making does not change what Claude costs, and the current published rate card is worth restating so the picture is complete:</p>

  <table>
    <thead>
      <tr><th>Model tier</th><th>Input /1M</th><th>Cached input /1M</th><th>Output /1M</th></tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td>Fable 5.1 / Mythos 5.1</td><td>$10</td><td>$0.25</td><td>$50</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Opus 5 / Opus 4.8</td><td>$5</td><td>$0.50</td><td>$25</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Sonnet 5 / Sonnet 4.5</td><td>$3</td><td>$0.30</td><td>$15</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Haiku 4.5</td><td>$0.80</td><td>$0.10</td><td>$4</td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>

  <p>The cache-read spread is the number that changed most recently and matters most for agent workloads: Fable 5.1's cached input rate fell to <strong>$0.25 per million</strong> from $1.00 in the prior generation, a 75% reduction. For a long-context agent that re-sends a stable system prompt on every step, that single field often dominates the bill far more than the headline input price does.</p>

  <h2 id="bottom-line">The Bottom Line</h2>
  <p>Anthropic's August 2026 report is unusual in this genre because it publishes the uncomfortable parts — including a case where the company's own classifier was bypassed by a fallback that Claude wrote. For developers, the directly actionable material is narrow and specific: account trust controls tied to new keys, an enforceable boundary around reasoning-trace capture, and the disclosure question that third-party gateways now answer badly.</p>

  <p>The rest of the report is context. It is genuinely useful context if you are deciding how much to route through a white-labelled endpoint, or if you are writing a security section for an internal model-vendor review. It is not a reason to change model choice on cost grounds, and it does not alter the published rate card.</p>

  <p>What it does establish is that the cheap-per-token tier is where abuse concentrates — not because smaller models are less safe, but because they are the economically rational target. That is a useful prior when you model attacker behaviour against your own product.</p>

  <ul>
    <li>Anthropic threat report window: December 2025 – August 2026, seven abuse categories</li>
    <li>Largest measured distillation campaign: 151M+ exchanges, May–July 2026 (Qwen, GTG-16005)</li>
    <li>Fable 5.1 "preserved thinking" is the control aimed at new API accounts</li>
    <li>Cached input on Fable 5.1: $0.25 / 1M — down from $1.00 in the prior generation</li>
    <li>Abuse clustered on Haiku, Sonnet, Opus; Fable and Mythos appeared once</li>
  </ul>
`;
